cellSpurs: wake an LFQueue waiter by the SPU library's own rule, and give each taskset a real workload id.

Games: A Crack in Time 1.20 (run acit-boot2): _LFQueueInitialize(owner = spurs|1 = 0x00FDF981, q = 0x00F77400, dir 3), the Edge
task 0 of taskset 0x409AA200 (the game's own taskset object, passed to CreateTasksetWithAttribute) waits on the empty queue, the
PPU push succeeds, and 7030 signalled "task 0 of taskset 0x00FDF980" (eaSignal & ~1 = the SPURS instance), so the task never woke
and the PPU blocked forever in cellSpursEventFlagWait. Tools of Destruction (owner = the taskset, dir 2) uses the same SPU code.

Wake rule, decoded from the game's SPU task library (ACiT Edge task elf_00be3800, ToD elf_008ea200 has the same code):
 - a waiting consumer stores id = (wklCurrentId << 8) | taskId in the ring (id callback @0x97F8, ToD @0x99E8:
   LS 0x1DC << 8 | u32 at [LS 0x2FB8] + 0xD4 = LS 0x27D4; SpursKernelContext.wklCurrentId, SpursTasksetContext.taskId);
 - fpSendSignal @0x99F8 (called @0xA798 with eaSignal = line +0x70): eaSignal & 15 == 0 -> eaSignal is the taskset;
   otherwise (IWL, owner = spurs | 1) wid = id >> 8 (> 15 -> 0x80410902), taskset = wklInfo1[wid].arg (@0x9778 DMAs
   spurs + 0xB00 + 32 * wid); then cellSpursSendSignal(taskset, id & 0xFF) (@0x9828: taskset 0 -> 0x80410911, taskset & 0x7F ->
   0x80410910, task > 127 -> 0x80410902).
 - console CreateTaskset adds a workload (taskset PM, argument = the taskset EA) and stores its id in taskset->wid (+0x74)
   [src:rpcs3-cellspurs: _spurs::create_taskset, cellSpursLookUpTasksetAddress, cellSpursEventFlagSet's IWL path].

Changes:
 - _cellSpursLFQueuePushBody: rc_lfq_wake routes the waiter by that rule (was: spu_taskset_signal_task(eaSignal & ~1, id));
   the first 8 wakes are logged "[cellSpurs] LFQueuePush wake q=... id=... -> taskset ... task ...".
 - spurs_create_taskset: reserves a workload id for the taskset (wklInfo1[wid].arg = taskset, taskset +0x74 = wid), without a
   policy module: wklEnabled/wklState stay 0 and the PM is marked missing, so the port kernel never selects it (the port runs
   taskset tasks itself, spu_workload.c). The same taskset re-created at its EA keeps its slot; JoinTaskset frees it.
   No free slot (the port's 16-slot table is shared by every instance) -> logged, wid stays 0 as before.
 - spurs_pm_build_context: LS 0x1DC (wklCurrentId) = taskset +0x74, as when the kernel runs the taskset PM. It was 0, so every
   task looked like a task of workload 0 (ACiT: Wws_Job SharedList0).

ASSUMPTIONs:
 - the PPU push in libsre routes a waiter like the SPU's fpSendSignal (no PPU push code exists in the EBOOT);
 - the spurs base for an IWL lookup is eaSignal & ~15 (the SPU uses its kernel context's spurs at LS 0x1C0, the same instance);
 - wklInfo1[wid].addr/size of a taskset workload stay 0 (console: the firmware taskset PM image).
Host test: port/rc/tests/test_lfqueue.c (case owner = spurs|1, waiter in the taskset of wid 2; fails on 7030 alone).

